1
00:00:00,112 --> 00:00:01,475
The ceiling is quieter

2
00:00:01,475 --> 00:00:03,193
than it has been in a month.

3
00:00:03,193 --> 00:00:05,403
Quieter is not the same as empty.

4
00:00:05,403 --> 00:00:07,030
Your name is Patch.

5
00:00:07,659 --> 00:00:08,518
Thirty days ago,

6
00:00:08,518 --> 00:00:10,944
you were waiting for a bus.

7
00:00:10,944 --> 00:00:13,093
Since then, you have lived in a concrete

8
00:00:13,093 --> 00:00:16,204
room under the depot, with a steel door,

9
00:00:16,204 --> 00:00:19,422
a bench, two racks of shrinking supplies,

10
00:00:19,422 --> 00:00:21,904
and Nora, who used to clean this building

11
00:00:21,904 --> 00:00:24,975
and now keeps both of you alive inside it.

12
00:00:24,975 --> 00:00:27,153
Yesterday, a truck crawled past the

13
00:00:27,153 --> 00:00:28,929
street opening and pulled a long

14
00:00:28,929 --> 00:00:31,470
stream of the horde away behind its engine.

15
00:00:31,470 --> 00:00:34,117
Somebody living was driving it.

16
00:00:34,651 --> 00:00:36,664
Nora is holding the flashlight.

17
00:00:36,664 --> 00:00:39,031
Your hand is on the orange bag.

18
00:00:39,031 --> 00:00:40,662
Day 31 has started,

19
00:00:40,662 --> 00:00:43,108
and neither of you has moved.

20
00:00:43,108 --> 00:00:45,743
"Upstairs first," she whispers.

21
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:47,207
"Then we decide."

22
00:00:47,207 --> 00:00:49,005
From the corridor window,

23
00:00:49,005 --> 00:00:51,561
the yard looks like a stadium after

24
00:00:51,561 --> 00:00:53,365
everyone important has gone home.

25
00:00:53,365 --> 00:00:55,099
A few dozen walkers still stand

26
00:00:55,099 --> 00:00:57,502
between the loading bay door and the street.

27
00:00:57,502 --> 00:01:00,076
Most of them are bunched around the coach.

28
00:01:00,076 --> 00:01:01,216
The truck is gone.

29
00:01:01,216 --> 00:01:02,422
So is its sound.

30
00:01:02,422 --> 00:01:03,989
You want to follow it.

31
00:01:03,989 --> 00:01:05,706
You say so quietly,

32
00:01:05,706 --> 00:01:08,632
because that is how all your arguments happen now.

33
00:01:08,632 --> 00:01:11,070
Nora points at the street opening.

34
00:01:11,070 --> 00:01:13,542
Then at the walkers between you and it.

35
00:01:13,965 --> 00:01:16,547
Then at the desk wedged against the bay door,

36
00:01:16,547 --> 00:01:19,249
which is the only lock you have left.

37
00:01:19,249 --> 00:01:20,585
Once that desk moves,

38
00:01:20,585 --> 00:01:24,189
nothing holds the door except whoever stays behind it.

39
00:01:24,189 --> 00:01:25,984
"Following an engine you can't hear

40
00:01:25,984 --> 00:01:28,616
anymore," she says, "is what they do."

41
00:01:28,616 --> 00:01:30,928
You look at the walkers in the yard,

42
00:01:30,928 --> 00:01:31,998
all still facing the

43
00:01:31,998 --> 00:01:34,329
street where the truck disappeared.

44
00:01:34,329 --> 00:01:35,225
She has a point.

45
00:01:35,225 --> 00:01:37,738
You would just be the best organized one.

46
00:01:38,046 --> 00:01:38,871
So you stay.

47
00:01:38,871 --> 00:01:41,711
But staying can no longer mean waiting.

48
00:01:42,036 --> 00:01:43,542
The world outside is changing,

49
00:01:43,542 --> 00:01:45,397
and you need to understand how.

50
00:01:46,108 --> 00:01:48,362
On day 32, you climb to the gantry.

51
00:01:48,362 --> 00:01:50,217
Without the loudspeaker,

52
00:01:50,217 --> 00:01:51,809
it is almost peaceful up there.

53
00:01:51,809 --> 00:01:53,829
You keep the flashlight off.

54
00:01:53,829 --> 00:01:55,041
Anything that moves

55
00:01:55,041 --> 00:01:56,919
above the yard gets looked at.

56
00:01:56,919 --> 00:01:58,376
Nora stops at the

57
00:01:58,376 --> 00:01:59,459
first turn of the stairs.

58
00:01:59,459 --> 00:02:01,694
Her hand finds the rail and stays there.

59
00:02:01,694 --> 00:02:04,277
Then she breathes out and keeps climbing.

60
00:02:04,277 --> 00:02:06,375
This time, you stay at the far end

61
00:02:06,375 --> 00:02:08,528
long enough to look over the depot wall.

62
00:02:08,998 --> 00:02:09,846
A street.

63
00:02:09,846 --> 00:02:13,429
Parked cars nobody will ever move again.

64
00:02:13,429 --> 00:02:15,445
A road climbing toward the hill.

65
00:02:15,899 --> 00:02:17,062
Somewhere beyond it,

66
00:02:17,062 --> 00:02:19,135
the man on the radio is hiding.

67
00:02:19,589 --> 00:02:21,156
And at the street opening,

68
00:02:21,156 --> 00:02:22,375
a heavy sliding gate,

69
00:02:22,375 --> 00:02:24,751
pushed all the way back against the wall.

70
00:02:24,751 --> 00:02:27,124
It has been open since the first day.

71
00:02:27,434 --> 00:02:29,861
Nobody stayed long enough to close it.

72
00:02:29,861 --> 00:02:31,123
Below you,

73
00:02:31,123 --> 00:02:33,741
the remaining walkers drift around the coach.

74
00:02:33,741 --> 00:02:36,375
One of them wears a blue depot vest.

75
00:02:36,375 --> 00:02:37,778
Nora looks at him for a

76
00:02:37,778 --> 00:02:39,855
long time and says nothing.

77
00:02:39,855 --> 00:02:41,788
You do not ask.

78
00:02:42,354 --> 00:02:45,896
Days 33 and 34 belong to the radio.

79
00:02:46,302 --> 00:02:48,743
The store gave you one box of batteries,

80
00:02:48,743 --> 00:02:50,437
and the radio eats them

81
00:02:50,437 --> 00:02:52,889
like it is being paid by the hour.

82
00:02:52,889 --> 00:02:55,744
You agree on five minutes at sunset.

83
00:02:55,744 --> 00:02:56,665
No more.

84
00:02:57,537 --> 00:03:00,930
On day 33, the man answers almost immediately,

85
00:03:00,930 --> 00:03:02,481
as if he has been

86
00:03:02,481 --> 00:03:04,356
sitting beside his speaker all week.

87
00:03:04,356 --> 00:03:05,669
He asks your names.

88
00:03:06,187 --> 00:03:07,269
You tell him.

89
00:03:07,269 --> 00:03:08,660
There is a pause.

90
00:03:08,660 --> 00:03:11,340
"Is Patch a name or an injury?"

91
00:03:11,340 --> 00:03:12,785
"Both," you say.

92
00:03:12,785 --> 00:03:14,862
He laughs very quietly,

93
00:03:14,862 --> 00:03:18,002
which tells you he has learned the same lessons you have.

94
00:03:18,002 --> 00:03:19,014
His name is Wes.

95
00:03:19,014 --> 00:03:20,979
Learning it this late feels like

96
00:03:20,979 --> 00:03:23,386
finding the instructions after you

97
00:03:23,386 --> 00:03:25,148
have already built the furniture.

98
00:03:25,148 --> 00:03:27,340
Wes has seen the truck twice.

99
00:03:27,340 --> 00:03:28,907
Both times in the morning.

100
00:03:28,907 --> 00:03:30,347
Both times slowly,

101
00:03:30,347 --> 00:03:33,312
with one short blast of the horn at every corner.

102
00:03:33,312 --> 00:03:35,369
Both times, a long tail of

103
00:03:35,369 --> 00:03:37,119
walkers followed it out of his

104
00:03:37,119 --> 00:03:39,344
street and did not come back.

105
00:03:39,344 --> 00:03:41,876
You think about that all through day 34.

106
00:03:41,876 --> 00:03:43,689
The people in that truck

107
00:03:43,689 --> 00:03:45,094
are not running from the dead.

108
00:03:45,094 --> 00:03:46,091
They are moving them.

109
00:03:46,091 --> 00:03:47,204
For a month,

110
00:03:47,204 --> 00:03:49,305
you have treated sound as the enemy.

111
00:03:49,305 --> 00:03:52,765
Somebody out there is using it as a tool.

112
00:03:52,765 --> 00:03:54,702
On days 35 and 36,

113
00:03:54,702 --> 00:03:57,237
Nora lays everything you own across the bench

114
00:03:57,237 --> 00:03:59,785
and does the arithmetic in a whisper.

115
00:03:59,785 --> 00:04:00,770
Eleven days of food if

116
00:04:00,770 --> 00:04:02,521
you eat like normal people.

117
00:04:02,831 --> 00:04:06,355
Fifteen if you eat like careful people.

118
00:04:06,355 --> 00:04:08,040
Twenty if you eat like people

119
00:04:08,040 --> 00:04:09,745
who are about to start arguing.

120
00:04:09,745 --> 00:04:10,934
You choose careful.

121
00:04:10,934 --> 00:04:12,392
By the second evening,

122
00:04:12,392 --> 00:04:15,548
your hands are slower and your temper is shorter.

123
00:04:15,548 --> 00:04:17,912
Nora drops the pencil twice.

124
00:04:17,912 --> 00:04:19,416
Both times, you freeze and stare

125
00:04:19,416 --> 00:04:21,456
at the ceiling until it stays quiet.

126
00:04:21,830 --> 00:04:24,382
Hunger, it turns out, is noisy too.

127
00:04:24,382 --> 00:04:25,872
The store is bare.

128
00:04:25,872 --> 00:04:27,358
The office is bare.

129
00:04:27,358 --> 00:04:30,249
There is no next easy supply room.

130
00:04:30,249 --> 00:04:32,295
But there is a next hard one.

131
00:04:32,686 --> 00:04:34,865
It has been parked in the middle of the

132
00:04:34,865 --> 00:04:37,675
yard since day one, with its door half open.

133
00:04:37,675 --> 00:04:38,400
A month ago,

134
00:04:38,400 --> 00:04:40,597
you were supposed to catch a bus.

135
00:04:40,955 --> 00:04:44,224
Now you are planning to rob one.

136
00:04:44,224 --> 00:04:47,241
Days 37 and 38 go into watching.

137
00:04:47,241 --> 00:04:49,690
From the corridor window,

138
00:04:49,690 --> 00:04:51,663
you learn the yard's habits.

139
00:04:51,663 --> 00:04:52,547
On dry days,

140
00:04:52,547 --> 00:04:55,507
the walkers spread out across the concrete.

141
00:04:55,507 --> 00:04:58,373
When it rains, they crowd under the

142
00:04:58,373 --> 00:05:01,759
canopy around the coach, as they have all month.

143
00:05:01,759 --> 00:05:04,147
So the coach needs a dry day.

144
00:05:04,697 --> 00:05:07,274
Then you make the rule that matters.

145
00:05:07,274 --> 00:05:09,133
You will go out alone.

146
00:05:09,586 --> 00:05:11,321
Nora will hold the bay door.

147
00:05:11,321 --> 00:05:14,245
If walkers come toward it, she closes it.

148
00:05:14,245 --> 00:05:16,864
Even if you are still outside.

149
00:05:16,864 --> 00:05:18,251
She refuses.

150
00:05:18,251 --> 00:05:19,532
You insist.

151
00:05:19,532 --> 00:05:22,898
She refuses again, more quietly.

152
00:05:22,898 --> 00:05:25,067
In the end she agrees,

153
00:05:25,067 --> 00:05:27,704
and does not speak to you for the rest of the evening,

154
00:05:27,704 --> 00:05:29,975
which is how you know she means it.

155
00:05:29,975 --> 00:05:32,032
You agree on one more thing.

156
00:05:32,032 --> 00:05:33,579
If you are ever separated,

157
00:05:33,579 --> 00:05:35,206
she will flash the light from

158
00:05:35,206 --> 00:05:37,769
the corridor window once an hour.

159
00:05:37,769 --> 00:05:38,728
One flash.

160
00:05:38,728 --> 00:05:40,075
Still here.

161
00:05:40,482 --> 00:05:42,832
Day 39 starts dry and grey.

162
00:05:42,832 --> 00:05:45,238
Clouds sit over the hill.

163
00:05:45,238 --> 00:05:46,420
Clouds are not rain,

164
00:05:46,420 --> 00:05:49,246
you tell yourself, with the confidence of someone

165
00:05:49,246 --> 00:05:51,508
who has spent five weeks underground.

166
00:05:51,946 --> 00:05:53,342
You and Nora slide the desk

167
00:05:53,342 --> 00:05:55,088
back a centimeter at a time.

168
00:05:55,479 --> 00:05:57,684
She opens the door just wide enough.

169
00:05:57,684 --> 00:05:59,573
Then you are outside.

170
00:05:59,573 --> 00:06:00,833
The air is enormous.

171
00:06:00,833 --> 00:06:03,534
The yard smells of diesel and old rain.

172
00:06:03,989 --> 00:06:06,975
You keep your shoulder to the wall and move slowly,

173
00:06:06,975 --> 00:06:08,766
steadily, without stopping.

174
00:06:08,766 --> 00:06:10,044
You are trying very

175
00:06:10,044 --> 00:06:11,803
hard not to be interesting.

176
00:06:11,803 --> 00:06:14,266
The nearest one is twenty steps away,

177
00:06:14,266 --> 00:06:15,675
facing the street.

178
00:06:15,675 --> 00:06:17,010
It does not turn.

179
00:06:17,010 --> 00:06:18,956
You climb into the coach and pull

180
00:06:18,956 --> 00:06:21,232
the folding door almost shut behind you.

181
00:06:21,670 --> 00:06:23,330
Inside, there is nobody,

182
00:06:23,330 --> 00:06:26,662
unless you count abandoned jackets and the small,

183
00:06:26,662 --> 00:06:29,246
sad leftovers of other people's journeys.

184
00:06:29,605 --> 00:06:32,201
You open the overhead racks one by one.

185
00:06:32,592 --> 00:06:33,428
Crackers.

186
00:06:33,428 --> 00:06:35,100
Two bottles of water.

187
00:06:35,100 --> 00:06:36,758
A bar of chocolate.

188
00:06:36,758 --> 00:06:38,109
A first-aid kit.

189
00:06:38,109 --> 00:06:40,115
You pack the orange bag without

190
00:06:40,115 --> 00:06:42,330
letting anything touch anything else.

191
00:06:42,769 --> 00:06:44,657
Then something taps the roof.

192
00:06:44,657 --> 00:06:46,278
Then something else.

193
00:06:46,781 --> 00:06:49,214
Then the whole coach becomes a drum.

194
00:06:49,214 --> 00:06:51,171
The rain does exactly what

195
00:06:51,171 --> 00:06:52,954
rain has always done here.

196
00:06:52,954 --> 00:06:54,855
The walkers come in under the

197
00:06:54,855 --> 00:06:56,769
canopy and pack around the coach,

198
00:06:56,769 --> 00:06:58,599
shoulders brushing its sides.

199
00:06:58,599 --> 00:06:59,829
You go flat in the

200
00:06:59,829 --> 00:07:01,749
aisle between the seats.

201
00:07:02,203 --> 00:07:04,609
A few of them drift toward the bay door.

202
00:07:04,609 --> 00:07:07,651
Through the windshield, you see it close.

203
00:07:07,651 --> 00:07:09,090
Nora kept the rule.

204
00:07:09,090 --> 00:07:10,484
You wanted her to.

205
00:07:10,484 --> 00:07:12,669
It still feels like being left

206
00:07:12,669 --> 00:07:14,964
on the wrong side of the world.

207
00:07:14,964 --> 00:07:17,363
The rain does one kind thing.

208
00:07:17,913 --> 00:07:19,520
It covers small sounds.

209
00:07:19,520 --> 00:07:21,343
So you keep working,

210
00:07:21,343 --> 00:07:23,453
slowly, in its rhythm.

211
00:07:23,453 --> 00:07:25,206
Dried fruit.

212
00:07:25,206 --> 00:07:25,822
Cookies.

213
00:07:25,822 --> 00:07:27,260
A tube of sunscreen,

214
00:07:27,260 --> 00:07:29,613
which feels optimistic.

215
00:07:30,181 --> 00:07:32,146
And a wind-up travel alarm clock,

216
00:07:32,146 --> 00:07:33,940
a device whose only purpose

217
00:07:33,940 --> 00:07:35,468
is to make a sudden loud noise.

218
00:07:35,468 --> 00:07:37,973
You hold it like it might be listening.

219
00:07:37,973 --> 00:07:39,131
In the first-aid kit,

220
00:07:39,131 --> 00:07:40,500
there is a fresh bandage.

221
00:07:40,500 --> 00:07:42,386
You change yours in the dark.

222
00:07:42,386 --> 00:07:43,493
After thirty-nine days,

223
00:07:43,493 --> 00:07:44,969
you have a new one.

224
00:07:44,969 --> 00:07:46,473
It is also orange.

225
00:07:46,473 --> 00:07:49,632
Apparently, that is just who you are now.

226
00:07:50,183 --> 00:07:51,770
Every hour, through the rain,

227
00:07:51,770 --> 00:07:54,723
a small light blinks once in the corridor window.

228
00:07:54,723 --> 00:07:55,750
Still here.

229
00:07:55,750 --> 00:07:59,075
On day 40, the rain stops at dawn.

230
00:07:59,075 --> 00:08:00,745
The walkers do not move.

231
00:08:00,745 --> 00:08:02,776
Of course they don't.

232
00:08:02,776 --> 00:08:05,156
They did not leave when the coach alarm stopped,

233
00:08:05,156 --> 00:08:05,982
and they are not

234
00:08:05,982 --> 00:08:08,195
leaving because the weather has improved.

235
00:08:08,195 --> 00:08:10,577
You press the orange bag against a

236
00:08:10,577 --> 00:08:13,089
window so Nora can see it, and you wait.

237
00:08:13,089 --> 00:08:16,298
Then something clinks high above the yard.

238
00:08:16,298 --> 00:08:18,608
On the gantry, a small figure with a

239
00:08:18,608 --> 00:08:21,144
purple band on her arm is throwing your trash.

240
00:08:21,486 --> 00:08:23,227
Nora has climbed up there alone,

241
00:08:23,227 --> 00:08:24,758
with a bag of the empty cans

242
00:08:24,758 --> 00:08:27,107
you spent a month never dropping.

243
00:08:27,107 --> 00:08:28,746
Her first can hits the top of

244
00:08:28,746 --> 00:08:30,997
the wall and falls back into the yard,

245
00:08:30,997 --> 00:08:32,522
near the street opening.

246
00:08:33,041 --> 00:08:34,147
Every head turns.

247
00:08:34,147 --> 00:08:36,667
Half the crowd shuffles toward it,

248
00:08:36,667 --> 00:08:38,009
away from the coach.

249
00:08:38,009 --> 00:08:40,553
Her second can sails clean over

250
00:08:40,553 --> 00:08:42,489
the wall and rattles down the street.

251
00:08:42,489 --> 00:08:43,508
The walkers nearest

252
00:08:43,508 --> 00:08:45,199
the opening follow it out.

253
00:08:45,199 --> 00:08:46,422
You slip out of the

254
00:08:46,422 --> 00:08:48,878
coach and move along the wall.

255
00:08:48,878 --> 00:08:51,216
At the bay door, you knock three times.

256
00:08:51,216 --> 00:08:53,835
Then a fourth time, softer.

257
00:08:53,835 --> 00:08:54,945
Thirty-six days ago,

258
00:08:54,945 --> 00:08:58,427
Nora knocked on your door exactly like that.

259
00:08:58,427 --> 00:08:59,762
You have been waiting a

260
00:08:59,762 --> 00:09:01,969
long time to return the joke.

261
00:09:01,969 --> 00:09:03,650
The door does not open.

262
00:09:04,104 --> 00:09:06,509
Nora is still running down the stairs.

263
00:09:06,509 --> 00:09:09,359
It is the longest minute of the month.

264
00:09:09,359 --> 00:09:10,629
Then the gap appears,

265
00:09:10,629 --> 00:09:12,957
and her hand pulls you inside.

266
00:09:12,957 --> 00:09:14,483
Her fingers are shaking.

267
00:09:14,483 --> 00:09:17,074
"I didn't look down," she says.

268
00:09:17,074 --> 00:09:18,188
"Not once."

269
00:09:18,188 --> 00:09:20,131
On days 41 and 42,

270
00:09:20,131 --> 00:09:22,605
you count what the coach paid.

271
00:09:22,605 --> 00:09:24,385
About ten more days of food,

272
00:09:24,385 --> 00:09:25,822
if you stay careful.

273
00:09:26,308 --> 00:09:26,886
Water.

274
00:09:26,886 --> 00:09:28,290
A clean bandage.

275
00:09:28,290 --> 00:09:30,514
It also charged a fee.

276
00:09:30,514 --> 00:09:33,169
A night in a wet coach gives you a cold,

277
00:09:33,543 --> 00:09:36,242
and your body invents a brand new alarm.

278
00:09:36,242 --> 00:09:38,190
You spend two days sneezing into

279
00:09:38,190 --> 00:09:40,709
the orange bag like it owes you money.

280
00:09:41,468 --> 00:09:42,745
And when Nora slammed the

281
00:09:42,745 --> 00:09:43,826
bay door in the rain,

282
00:09:43,826 --> 00:09:46,247
the crooked latch finally snapped off.

283
00:09:46,247 --> 00:09:47,500
Now the door cannot even

284
00:09:47,500 --> 00:09:49,455
pretend to stay shut on its own.

285
00:09:49,909 --> 00:09:51,304
You fetch the trolley you

286
00:09:51,304 --> 00:09:52,754
abandoned upstairs three weeks

287
00:09:52,754 --> 00:09:54,941
ago and wedge it behind the desk.

288
00:09:54,941 --> 00:09:57,094
It squeaks once, in protest.

289
00:09:57,452 --> 00:09:59,863
You both stare at it until it stops.

290
00:09:59,863 --> 00:10:01,356
But you have learned

291
00:10:01,356 --> 00:10:04,059
something worth more than crackers.

292
00:10:04,513 --> 00:10:06,466
The walkers that followed the cans

293
00:10:06,466 --> 00:10:08,894
into the street have not come back.

294
00:10:08,894 --> 00:10:09,899
For a month,

295
00:10:09,899 --> 00:10:13,519
the rule that they don't leave when the noise

296
00:10:13,519 --> 00:10:15,385
stops kept them on your ceiling.

297
00:10:15,385 --> 00:10:18,257
Now it keeps them on the street.

298
00:10:18,257 --> 00:10:20,726
From day 43 to day 46,

299
00:10:20,726 --> 00:10:23,910
you thin the yard one can at a time.

300
00:10:23,910 --> 00:10:25,537
Every dry morning,

301
00:10:25,537 --> 00:10:28,185
you climb to the gantry together.

302
00:10:28,511 --> 00:10:29,462
Nora throws.

303
00:10:29,462 --> 00:10:31,962
She is better at it than you,

304
00:10:31,962 --> 00:10:34,954
and she has decided this is not up for discussion.

305
00:10:34,954 --> 00:10:36,860
You watch and count.

306
00:10:36,860 --> 00:10:39,721
Two walkers out on day 43.

307
00:10:40,222 --> 00:10:42,330
Five on day 44.

308
00:10:42,330 --> 00:10:44,738
Three on day 45.

309
00:10:45,336 --> 00:10:46,566
On the bunker wall,

310
00:10:46,566 --> 00:10:48,596
your chart grows a second column:

311
00:10:48,596 --> 00:10:51,584
days on the left, walkers gone on the right.

312
00:10:51,989 --> 00:10:53,345
Empty cans become your

313
00:10:53,345 --> 00:10:54,877
most valuable possession.

314
00:10:54,877 --> 00:10:56,077
You suggest eating

315
00:10:56,077 --> 00:10:58,359
faster to make more ammunition.

316
00:10:58,359 --> 00:10:59,636
Nora explains,

317
00:10:59,636 --> 00:11:03,068
slowly, why that is not how food works.

318
00:11:03,068 --> 00:11:04,757
Then, on day 46,

319
00:11:04,757 --> 00:11:07,724
the right-hand column stops growing.

320
00:11:07,724 --> 00:11:08,580
You throw.

321
00:11:08,580 --> 00:11:09,513
They leave.

322
00:11:09,513 --> 00:11:12,506
And new ones wander in from the street,

323
00:11:12,506 --> 00:11:13,982
through the open gate.

324
00:11:13,982 --> 00:11:17,088
The yard is a bathtub with no plug.

325
00:11:17,088 --> 00:11:18,906
On days 47 to 49,

326
00:11:18,906 --> 00:11:22,113
the gate becomes the only thing you talk about.

327
00:11:22,113 --> 00:11:24,423
A heavy sliding panel on a rail,

328
00:11:24,423 --> 00:11:27,505
with a bolt that drops into a hole in the ground.

329
00:11:27,505 --> 00:11:29,546
Nora knows it screams when it moves,

330
00:11:29,546 --> 00:11:31,009
because she spent

331
00:11:31,009 --> 00:11:32,612
years hearing it every evening.

332
00:11:32,612 --> 00:11:33,765
That scream will reach

333
00:11:33,765 --> 00:11:35,526
the yard and the street.

334
00:11:35,526 --> 00:11:36,840
Everything in both places

335
00:11:36,840 --> 00:11:39,147
will come to see what made it.

336
00:11:39,147 --> 00:11:41,128
So you plan around the scream.

337
00:11:41,128 --> 00:11:43,383
You add the gate to the corridor sketch.

338
00:11:43,383 --> 00:11:45,543
You walk the route in your head

339
00:11:45,543 --> 00:11:47,740
until you can do it with your eyes closed.

340
00:11:47,740 --> 00:11:50,766
Nora says that is a bad habit to practice.

341
00:11:50,766 --> 00:11:51,898
She will be on the

342
00:11:51,898 --> 00:11:53,737
gantry with a bag of cans.

343
00:11:53,737 --> 00:11:55,723
You will carry the sunscreen.

344
00:11:55,723 --> 00:11:57,656
You intend to squeeze the

345
00:11:57,656 --> 00:11:59,157
whole tube onto the gate wheel.

346
00:11:59,157 --> 00:12:00,799
If nothing else, it will be the

347
00:12:00,799 --> 00:12:02,862
best protected gate in the city.

348
00:12:02,862 --> 00:12:03,857
Day 50 is dry,

349
00:12:03,857 --> 00:12:06,848
and you check the sky three times to make sure.

350
00:12:07,156 --> 00:12:09,871
Nora's first can lands behind the coach.

351
00:12:09,871 --> 00:12:11,331
The walkers left in

352
00:12:11,331 --> 00:12:13,127
the yard drift toward it.

353
00:12:13,127 --> 00:12:13,956
You go out.

354
00:12:13,956 --> 00:12:16,283
You move along the wall to the gate,

355
00:12:16,283 --> 00:12:19,327
empty the sunscreen onto the wheel and push.

356
00:12:19,327 --> 00:12:22,348
The sunscreen helps the way it always has.

357
00:12:22,707 --> 00:12:23,455
A little.

358
00:12:23,455 --> 00:12:24,353
Not enough.

359
00:12:24,353 --> 00:12:26,690
The gate lets out a long metal

360
00:12:26,690 --> 00:12:29,413
scream that seems to last a full minute.

361
00:12:29,948 --> 00:12:32,801
In the yard, every head turns toward you.

362
00:12:33,384 --> 00:12:34,351
Out in the street,

363
00:12:34,351 --> 00:12:37,554
figures you had not even noticed start walking your way.

364
00:12:37,976 --> 00:12:39,087
You push harder.

365
00:12:39,087 --> 00:12:40,199
The gap narrows.

366
00:12:40,199 --> 00:12:42,007
A hand appears in it,

367
00:12:42,007 --> 00:12:45,029
searching, exactly like on day 18.

368
00:12:45,029 --> 00:12:46,496
It slides back out a

369
00:12:46,496 --> 00:12:48,153
second before the gate closes.

370
00:12:48,153 --> 00:12:49,468
You drop the bolt.

371
00:12:49,859 --> 00:12:50,489
Behind you,

372
00:12:50,489 --> 00:12:53,097
the walkers in the yard are halfway across.

373
00:12:53,536 --> 00:12:56,342
Above, a can bangs off the canopy roof.

374
00:12:56,342 --> 00:12:58,729
Another clatters behind the coach.

375
00:12:58,729 --> 00:13:01,002
They hesitate, then turn.

376
00:13:01,002 --> 00:13:02,736
You run along the wall.

377
00:13:02,736 --> 00:13:04,626
Three knocks, one softer.

378
00:13:04,936 --> 00:13:05,798
The door opens.

379
00:13:05,798 --> 00:13:07,360
On the far side of the gate,

380
00:13:07,360 --> 00:13:08,985
fists begin hitting metal.

381
00:13:08,985 --> 00:13:09,914
For fifty days,

382
00:13:09,914 --> 00:13:13,216
you have listened to walkers trying to get to you.

383
00:13:13,216 --> 00:13:14,589
This is the first time you

384
00:13:14,589 --> 00:13:16,724
get to listen to them fail.

385
00:13:16,724 --> 00:13:20,316
The gate holds through days 51, 52 and 53.

386
00:13:20,316 --> 00:13:23,312
The pounding does not stop.

387
00:13:23,895 --> 00:13:26,250
A new crowd now stands in the street,

388
00:13:26,250 --> 00:13:29,408
held back by a gate instead of your ceiling.

389
00:13:29,408 --> 00:13:31,599
At sunset on day 51,

390
00:13:31,599 --> 00:13:34,531
Wes tells you he is down to two cans of soup.

391
00:13:35,049 --> 00:13:36,742
The next time the truck passes,

392
00:13:36,742 --> 00:13:39,572
he is going up on his roof with a mirror.

393
00:13:39,572 --> 00:13:41,416
"If they're herding," he says,

394
00:13:41,416 --> 00:13:44,759
"I'm going to be a very obvious sheep."

395
00:13:44,759 --> 00:13:47,732
On day 52, he does not answer.

396
00:13:47,732 --> 00:13:50,976
On day 53, he does not answer.

397
00:13:50,976 --> 00:13:52,500
You leave the radio on

398
00:13:52,500 --> 00:13:54,391
longer than you agreed.

399
00:13:54,797 --> 00:13:56,868
Finally, Nora switches it off.

400
00:13:56,868 --> 00:13:57,870
"We're spending

401
00:13:57,870 --> 00:14:00,435
batteries on a silence," she says.

402
00:14:00,435 --> 00:14:02,878
Neither of you says out loud

403
00:14:02,878 --> 00:14:04,912
what the silence might mean.

404
00:14:04,912 --> 00:14:07,441
Eleven walkers are still inside the yard:

405
00:14:07,441 --> 00:14:09,638
the slow ones, the stubborn ones.

406
00:14:09,638 --> 00:14:13,019
Cans make them shuffle away from the coach,

407
00:14:13,019 --> 00:14:14,891
and then they shuffle back.

408
00:14:14,891 --> 00:14:17,276
On day 54, you realize you need them

409
00:14:17,276 --> 00:14:19,422
somewhere they cannot come back from.

410
00:14:19,422 --> 00:14:21,343
You look at the coach.

411
00:14:21,343 --> 00:14:23,506
Then at the travel alarm clock.

412
00:14:23,506 --> 00:14:26,025
Nora watches you look at both.

413
00:14:26,025 --> 00:14:27,575
"No," she says.

414
00:14:28,254 --> 00:14:28,637
Then,

415
00:14:28,637 --> 00:14:31,813
a minute later: "How long can you set it for?"

416
00:14:32,443 --> 00:14:34,869
On day 55, you walk out to the coach,

417
00:14:34,869 --> 00:14:37,563
open its door wide, wind the clock to five

418
00:14:37,563 --> 00:14:40,117
minutes and wedge it under the back seat.

419
00:14:40,522 --> 00:14:42,821
Then you hide behind a canopy pillar.

420
00:14:42,821 --> 00:14:45,238
Five minutes is a very long time.

421
00:14:45,238 --> 00:14:46,628
When the clock rings,

422
00:14:46,628 --> 00:14:48,622
it is a tiny, furious sound,

423
00:14:48,622 --> 00:14:51,599
and every walker in the yard turns toward it

424
00:14:51,599 --> 00:14:53,413
as if it has called their name.

425
00:14:53,413 --> 00:14:54,036
One by one,

426
00:14:54,036 --> 00:14:56,755
they climb the step into the coach.

427
00:14:56,755 --> 00:14:58,059
In an orderly line.

428
00:14:58,059 --> 00:15:00,042
Better behaved than most

429
00:15:00,042 --> 00:15:02,062
passengers you have ever traveled with.

430
00:15:02,062 --> 00:15:03,226
The man in the blue

431
00:15:03,226 --> 00:15:04,870
depot vest boards last.

432
00:15:04,870 --> 00:15:06,512
Nora has told you where

433
00:15:06,512 --> 00:15:08,096
the outside door release is.

434
00:15:08,096 --> 00:15:10,424
She cleaned these coaches for years.

435
00:15:10,424 --> 00:15:11,618
You slip forward,

436
00:15:11,618 --> 00:15:15,270
push the folding door shut and pull the lever down.

437
00:15:15,270 --> 00:15:17,221
The clock rings itself out.

438
00:15:17,562 --> 00:15:19,526
Eleven passengers stay aboard.

439
00:15:19,526 --> 00:15:22,740
They do not leave when the noise stops.

440
00:15:22,740 --> 00:15:24,072
They never do.

441
00:15:24,072 --> 00:15:25,437
On day 56,

442
00:15:25,437 --> 00:15:28,784
Nora finally talks about the man in the vest.

443
00:15:28,784 --> 00:15:30,981
He drove the night route, she says.

444
00:15:30,981 --> 00:15:32,778
He used to say the coach was the

445
00:15:32,778 --> 00:15:34,859
only quiet place in the whole depot.

446
00:15:35,345 --> 00:15:37,506
She looks at it for a while.

447
00:15:37,506 --> 00:15:38,998
"Well," she says.

448
00:15:39,452 --> 00:15:41,235
"He's got it now."

449
00:15:41,235 --> 00:15:43,787
On day 57, you stand in the

450
00:15:43,787 --> 00:15:45,889
middle of the yard in full daylight,

451
00:15:45,889 --> 00:15:47,773
and nothing turns toward you.

452
00:15:47,773 --> 00:15:48,945
Your knees do not

453
00:15:48,945 --> 00:15:50,819
know what to do with that.

454
00:15:50,819 --> 00:15:52,653
The coach sits with its

455
00:15:52,653 --> 00:15:54,557
eleven quiet passengers.

456
00:15:55,011 --> 00:15:57,045
The gate rattles now and then.

457
00:15:57,045 --> 00:15:58,919
Everything else is sky.

458
00:15:59,501 --> 00:16:01,295
But the truck has not come back,

459
00:16:01,295 --> 00:16:02,736
and neither has Wes.

460
00:16:03,189 --> 00:16:04,905
If the living are herding the dead,

461
00:16:04,905 --> 00:16:06,825
they are doing it somewhere else.

462
00:16:07,134 --> 00:16:09,356
So you decide to become easy to find.

463
00:16:09,356 --> 00:16:11,967
Nora brings out the cardboard she has

464
00:16:11,967 --> 00:16:13,655
been flattening since the first week.

465
00:16:13,655 --> 00:16:15,361
In the maintenance cupboard,

466
00:16:15,361 --> 00:16:17,126
you find the yellow paint once

467
00:16:17,126 --> 00:16:19,239
used to tell buses where to stop.

468
00:16:19,239 --> 00:16:21,819
You paint two words and a number:

469
00:16:22,233 --> 00:16:24,968
TWO ALIVE, and the radio channel.

470
00:16:26,250 --> 00:16:28,633
On day 58, you hang it from the far

471
00:16:28,633 --> 00:16:31,684
end of the gantry, over the street.

472
00:16:31,684 --> 00:16:33,565
Nora watches it sway.

473
00:16:33,565 --> 00:16:35,259
"A sign is a door you

474
00:16:35,259 --> 00:16:37,424
can't close," she says.

475
00:16:37,424 --> 00:16:38,365
She is right.

476
00:16:38,365 --> 00:16:39,777
Anybody can read it.

477
00:16:39,777 --> 00:16:41,691
You leave it up anyway.

478
00:16:42,544 --> 00:16:43,240
On day 59,

479
00:16:43,240 --> 00:16:46,402
you open the luggage hold under the coach.

480
00:16:46,402 --> 00:16:47,597
Eleven passengers watch

481
00:16:47,597 --> 00:16:49,090
you through the windows.

482
00:16:49,090 --> 00:16:50,764
None of them gets off.

483
00:16:51,138 --> 00:16:52,158
Inside the hold,

484
00:16:52,158 --> 00:16:55,099
there are suitcases, mostly full of clothes.

485
00:16:55,473 --> 00:16:56,750
And a cardboard box of

486
00:16:56,750 --> 00:16:58,550
somebody's wedding favors:

487
00:16:58,925 --> 00:17:02,296
two hundred tiny bags of sugared almonds.

488
00:17:02,296 --> 00:17:03,298
Somewhere out there,

489
00:17:03,298 --> 00:17:06,077
two people got married and never got to hand these out.

490
00:17:06,077 --> 00:17:08,426
Now their wedding is feeding you.

491
00:17:08,426 --> 00:17:11,165
That evening, you look up at the canopy.

492
00:17:11,165 --> 00:17:12,924
The solar panel is charging the

493
00:17:12,924 --> 00:17:15,482
loudspeaker battery in the last of the sun.

494
00:17:15,482 --> 00:17:16,532
The switch in the

495
00:17:16,532 --> 00:17:18,535
control box still says OFF.

496
00:17:18,535 --> 00:17:20,346
You leave it alone.

497
00:17:20,346 --> 00:17:22,984
Some promises are worth keeping,

498
00:17:22,984 --> 00:17:24,524
even to a loudspeaker.

499
00:17:24,524 --> 00:17:26,354
Near dawn on day 60,

500
00:17:26,354 --> 00:17:28,854
an engine sounds at the bottom of the street.

501
00:17:29,277 --> 00:17:31,197
The pounding on the gate stops.

502
00:17:31,197 --> 00:17:32,715
From the gantry,

503
00:17:32,715 --> 00:17:35,274
you watch a white truck crawl around the corner.

504
00:17:35,274 --> 00:17:37,315
One short blast of the horn.

505
00:17:37,315 --> 00:17:38,797
The crowd outside the gate

506
00:17:38,797 --> 00:17:41,120
peels away and follows it out of

507
00:17:41,120 --> 00:17:42,885
sight in a long, ragged line.

508
00:17:42,885 --> 00:17:44,458
Twenty minutes later,

509
00:17:44,458 --> 00:17:47,905
the engine comes back alone and stops outside your gate.

510
00:17:48,392 --> 00:17:51,148
The radio, silent for a week, crackles.

511
00:17:51,667 --> 00:17:52,180
"Depot.

512
00:17:52,180 --> 00:17:53,134
This is Wes.

513
00:17:53,134 --> 00:17:53,940
Nice sign.

514
00:17:53,940 --> 00:17:56,367
Your handwriting's terrible."

515
00:17:57,127 --> 00:17:59,456
They saw his mirror on day 52.

516
00:17:59,456 --> 00:18:02,222
His batteries died the same afternoon.

517
00:18:02,222 --> 00:18:03,527
He has spent a week

518
00:18:03,527 --> 00:18:05,921
convincing strangers that two people

519
00:18:05,921 --> 00:18:07,872
are living under a bus depot.

520
00:18:07,872 --> 00:18:10,228
Then a second voice comes on,

521
00:18:10,228 --> 00:18:13,125
a woman's, calm, used to being listened to.

522
00:18:13,740 --> 00:18:15,150
"Wes says there are two of you.

523
00:18:15,150 --> 00:18:16,318
How many of them are on

524
00:18:16,318 --> 00:18:18,252
your side of the gate?"

525
00:18:18,252 --> 00:18:19,928
You look at the coach.

526
00:18:19,928 --> 00:18:21,349
"Eleven," you say.

527
00:18:21,349 --> 00:18:22,277
"All seated."

528
00:18:22,277 --> 00:18:25,204
There is a long pause on the channel.

529
00:18:25,204 --> 00:18:27,792
"Open the gate," she says.

530
00:18:27,792 --> 00:18:28,585
"We need to talk

531
00:18:28,585 --> 00:18:30,322
before the crowd comes back."

532
00:18:31,305 --> 00:18:33,262
Nora is holding the flashlight.

533
00:18:33,262 --> 00:18:35,481
Your hand is on the orange bag.

534
00:18:35,481 --> 00:18:36,472
Sixty days.

535
00:18:36,472 --> 00:18:39,527
A new bandage, same color.

536
00:18:39,527 --> 00:18:42,155
Food for a few more weeks,

537
00:18:42,155 --> 00:18:43,701
most of it sugared almonds.

538
00:18:43,701 --> 00:18:45,777
A yard you emptied with your trash,

539
00:18:45,777 --> 00:18:49,123
and a coach that finally took some passengers.

540
00:18:49,123 --> 00:18:50,424
For sixty days,

541
00:18:50,424 --> 00:18:52,913
every door you closed kept the dead out.

542
00:18:52,913 --> 00:18:54,642
Tomorrow is day 61.

543
00:18:54,642 --> 00:18:57,720
Somebody alive is standing at your gate.

544
00:18:57,720 --> 00:18:58,891
And you have to decide

545
00:18:58,891 --> 00:19:00,990
whether this is a door you open.

546
00:19:00,990 --> 00:19:02,125
Would you open the gate?

547
00:19:02,125 --> 00:19:03,078
Which decision would

548
00:19:03,078 --> 00:19:04,604
you have made differently?

549
00:19:04,983 --> 00:19:06,323
Tell us in the comments,

550
00:19:06,323 --> 00:19:10,509
and subscribe to Last Safe Door, so you're here for day 61.
